  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nBroad upper-level ridging, with a surface high to our east, will \nallow for temperatures to climb to slightly above normal values \nMonday. Upper-level ridging becomes more amplified by the middle of \nthe week with increasing southerly flow helping temperatures \nwarm back to well above normal levels for this time of year. For\nreference, normal highs are generally in the middle 60s to \nstart the week, and fall into the lower to middle 60s by the \nweekend. With highs expected to climb into the mid to upper 60s \nMonday, we'll be a few degrees above normal. But as the upper \nridging amplifies mid-late week, we'll see highs climb into the \nupper 70s to lower 80s. So we should easily be 10-15 degrees \nabove normal and approaching 20 degrees above normal by late \nthis week and into the weekend. \n\nWith the upper-level ridging prevailing through the middle of the \ncountry, the forecast remains dry through the week. It's not \nuntil the weekend, when the ridge axis may shift sufficiently \nfar enough east that some precipitation chances start to \nencroach on the area. Models show a front pushing into \nnorthwestern MO and northeastern KS late Saturday into Sunday. \nThat front will provide a forcing mechanism for showers before \nwashing out and lifting northward again. There is some \nuncertainty in this though. About 45% of 500mb clusters show the\nridge axis roughly splitting Missouri. The remaining clusters \nare either further east or weaker overall with the ridging by \nlate in the week to the weekend. Stronger ridging that is \nfurther west would inhibit precipitation chances. Weaker ridging\nor further east ridging would allow precipitation chances to \nshift further east.",
